      "id": "aapl-risk-suppliers",
      "type": "narrative",
      "answer": "In its fiscal 2025 10-K, Apple says a significant majority of its manufacturing is done by outsourcing partners. These partners are mainly in China mainland, India, Japan, South Korea, Taiwan and Vietnam. Apple relies on single-source partners in the U.S., Asia and Europe for many components. It also relies on partners mainly in Asia for final assembly of substantially all hardware products, and it has outsourced much of its logistics. Apple says this lowers costs but reduces its direct control. That reduced control has at times hurt, and may again hurt, the cost, quality or quantity of products and its flexibility to respond to change. Apple also generally stays responsible to consumers for warranty service and defect liabilities. Supplier code-of-conduct violations occur from time to time and could materially harm its business. Because some components come from single or limited sources, Apple faces significant supply and pricing risks, including shortages such as the past semiconductor shortage. Changes to its supply chain also take considerable time and resources and carry significant risks.",
      "found": true,
      "value": null,
